# RedsRacing Cursor Rules

## Before adding helpers
- Always search the codebase for an existing helper that does the same job before creating a new one.
- Prefer extending a shared utility in `assets/js/`, Android common helpers, or iOS shared helpers over copy-pasting.
- If a near-duplicate exists across web / Android / iOS, consolidate or document why platform-specific copies must diverge.

## Null safety (strict)
- Never use force-unwraps or blind casts:
  - Kotlin: no `!!`
  - Swift: no `!` force unwraps (prefer `guard let`, `if let`, `??`)
  - TypeScript / JS: no `as any`, no unchecked non-null assertions (`!`), avoid `any` for public APIs
- Prefer explicit null/undefined checks, early returns, and typed fallbacks.
- Treat API payloads as untrusted: validate required fields before use.

## Network & API handling
- Every network call must handle failure paths (timeouts, non-2xx, parse errors, offline).
- Do not swallow errors silently; log with context and surface a user-safe message when UI-facing.
- Keep response handling consistent: same status codes, error shapes, and auth-refresh behavior across Web, Android, and iOS.
- Never assume a successful body shape; guard missing fields.

## Cross-platform parity
- Business logic, data models, and API contracts must stay aligned between website, Android, and iOS.
- Auth token storage, session refresh, and deep-link handling must be reviewed together when one platform changes.

## Performance & leaks
- Clean up listeners, observers, WebView callbacks, and streams in teardown / `onDestroy` / `deinit`.
- Avoid retaining Activity / ViewController / DOM references past lifecycle.
- Prefer cancelable fetches and structured concurrency over fire-and-forget network work.

## Audit / review mindset
- Flag dead code, unused imports, orphaned mirrored `www` copies that diverge from canonical sources.
- Prefer fixing canonical web sources then `npm run sync:mobile` — do not hand-edit Android/iOS mirrored bundles unless native-only.
